From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: hbadi <badi.hamid@gmail.com>
Date: Thu, 6 Aug 2026 15:50:59 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 6/6] Emit the Visual Studio artefacts for MSVC, not for
 Windows

The property sheet and the NuGet package were guarded on WIN32. MinGW is
Windows too, so a MinGW build produced a Visual Studio property sheet naming
.dll.a import libraries and its own header layout, which no Visual Studio
project can use. Guard on MSVC, which is what both artefacts are for.

(cherry picked from commit 841c0067b0b2ee03b789f61330998b307f37fde0)
---
 src/CMakeLists.txt | 7 ++++++-
 1 file changed, 6 insertions(+), 1 deletion(-)

diff --git a/src/CMakeLists.txt b/src/CMakeLists.txt
index 3e43fa8c..ffb4bca6 100644
--- a/src/CMakeLists.txt
+++ b/src/CMakeLists.txt
@@ -168,7 +168,12 @@ message(STATUS "Runtime helper .... ${_env_script}")
 # path. Generated from the variables just computed, so it cannot drift from the
 # CMake target. This is also the file the NuGet package ships as
 # build/native/sparselizard.props.
-if(WIN32)
+#
+# MSVC and not WIN32: MinGW is Windows too, and would otherwise be handed a
+# property sheet naming .dll.a import libraries and its own header paths, which
+# no Visual Studio project can use. Same for the NuGet package below, whose
+# whole content is MSVC specific.
+if(MSVC)
     include("${CMAKE_SOURCE_DIR}/cMake/writepropertysheet.cmake")
     sparselizard_write_property_sheet(
         "${CMAKE_RUNTIME_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY}/sparselizard.props"
